    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 16 with a lineup anchored by the high-upside profile of **Junior Caminero** (70.80) and **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (64.80). **Junior Caminero** benefits from a favorable matchup against Alcantara (4.46 xERA), allowing his raw power to shine despite a neutral park environment. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** remains our premier weapon for the stolen base category; despite hitting 8th, his aggressive platoon advantage against Martin and the projected opportunity to exploit the catcher's efficiency profile makes him an elite start today. **Luke Raley** (62.62) is another standout, capitalizing on the \"Slow SP Delivery\" factor against Buehler, which significantly boosts his stolen base projection in a contest where every efficiency point is precious.\n\nOur most difficult personnel decision centered on **Hyeseong Kim** (47.96), who fell just shy of cracking the starting nine. While **Hyeseong Kim** offers a solid floor, the optimizer prioritized the higher order-dependent upside of **Chase Meidroth** and the specific positional needs at MI/SS. We are also closely monitoring the lineup for **Casey Schmitt** (63.92); though his status is currently pending, his potential placement in the 3-hole against Severino (4.63 xERA) provides a substantial boost, making him a must-start if the projected batting order holds.\n\nThe primary risk today involves the top of our lineup, specifically **Yordan Alvarez** (54.65) and **Trea Turner** (59.13), both of whom face the daunting task of squaring off against deGrom and Chandler. While **Yordan Alvarez** maintains an elite 0.425 xwOBA, the 19% penalty assigned for the elite pitching matchup with deGrom suppresses his ceiling. Similarly, **Trea Turner** faces a challenge against Chandler\u2019s elite defensive pop-time, which necessitates a more cautious approach to our base-stealing expectations despite his favorable lead-off spot. We are holding **Christian Yelich**, **Colt Keith**, and 